WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:10.350
- I'm truly delighted
today to host Lan Duong

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.140
who is a pathbreaking scholar

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:15.930
working at the intersections
of cinema studies,

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:20.930
Asian American studies,
Vietnamese studies, Asian studies,

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:25.700
and critical diaspora studies,
critical refugee studies,

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:28.320
and truly is one of the
most important voices

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.570
at the intersections of all these fields.

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:33.780
Dr. Duong is an Associate Professor

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:35.990
in Cinema and Media Studies

00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:38.610
at the University of Southern California

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.703
and the author of "Treacherous Subjects:

00:00:40.703 --> 00:00:44.333
Gender, Culture, and
Trans-Vietnamese Feminism,"

00:00:45.436 --> 00:00:48.150
and a wide range of
important journal articles

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:49.559
and book chapters.

00:00:49.559 --> 00:00:51.620
I just wanna say a word about her book.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:53.580
It's truly pathbreaking.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:56.868
It introduces new ways of
intersectional analysis

00:00:56.868 --> 00:01:00.870
that break down all sorts
of disciplinary boundaries.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:02.750
It not only critiques the legacies

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:05.150
of colonialism and imperialism,

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:08.230
but takes on the fraught
challenge of engaging

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:10.890
with ideologies of traditionalism,

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:14.650
gender hierarchies, nationalism,

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:17.190
and does this all through
the provocative concept

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:20.470
of treachery as expressed
in both Vietnamese

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:23.810
and diasporic representational practices

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:25.313
and lived experience.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.830
For the sake of this gathering today

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:32.680
in the context of a council
on Southeast Asian studies,

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:36.170
I wanna signal how important the book is,

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:39.010
not only as a work of cinema studies,

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:42.620
but for Lan's a complete scholarly project

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:44.068
being especially important

00:01:44.068 --> 00:01:46.440
for the way in which it transcends

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.430
a deeply problematic fictional divide,

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:51.900
that for so long has separated scholars

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.660
working in Asian studies

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:56.680
from those working in
Asian American studies.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.457
For concept of trans-Vietnamese feminism

00:01:59.457 --> 00:02:01.620
and her method of studying both Vietnam

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.940
and the Vietnamese diaspora

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.429
within what she calls the
same coordinates of time

00:02:06.429 --> 00:02:08.613
and space, for example,

00:02:08.613 --> 00:02:11.760
is truly a clarion call for more work

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.140
that pushes beyond the
methodological nationalism

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:18.370
of area studies and also the US centrism

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:19.923
of so many other fields.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:24.010
I suspect that the title of today's talk,

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:27.460
On Water is part of this way of thinking,

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:29.870
thinking outside the little
buckets of scholarship

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:31.190
we tend to think with.

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:32.755
I like to think of water as something

00:02:32.755 --> 00:02:34.780
that we pour into buckets,

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:37.376
and it sometimes takes the
shape of those buckets.

00:02:37.376 --> 00:02:39.620
But if we jostle the buckets,

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:43.620
the water can leap out beyond
the bounds of those buckets

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:46.040
and mingle with the
waters of other buckets

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:47.870
and take on new shapes.

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:50.580
So I'm excited to see what happens today

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.040
when Professor Duong shakes things up

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.620
and gives us new ways of looking
at trans-Vietnamese cinema,

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:59.590
social life, politics, and history.

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:03.006
So please join me in
welcoming professor Lan Duong.

00:03:03.006 --> 00:03:04.214
(Erik claps)

00:03:04.214 --> 00:03:05.223
- Thank you.

00:03:05.223 --> 00:03:08.461
Thank you, Erik for that
wonderful introduction.

00:03:08.461 --> 00:03:13.340
It sounds like both you
and my mom read the book,

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.240
so that's very nice. (chuckles)

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:16.810
Kidding.

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:20.440
But I wanted to say thank
you to Erik as well.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:22.810
And Quan Tran for inviting me

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:24.890
and being a part of this space.

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:26.540
It's really an honor,

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:31.540
and a pleasure to talk to you
today about my talk On Water

00:03:31.663 --> 00:03:36.663
and refugee memories in
Vietnamese diasporic films.

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:38.637
So I'm gonna share screen now.

00:03:40.424 --> 00:03:45.424
And I begin with Lin and
Lam's very beautiful image

00:03:46.373 --> 00:03:49.280
called Tomorrow I Leave.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.810
And as you can see, there is
from what I'm sketching out,

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:57.122
a predominance or
preoccupation with water,

00:03:57.122 --> 00:04:01.660
both in art and films of the Vietnamese

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:03.093
and Vietnamese diaspora.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:10.450
And so today in terms
of films, especially,

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:14.380
I'm gonna be talking about Tran Anh Hung's

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:15.893
La Pierre de l'Attente,

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:19.593
Khoa Do's Mother Fish,

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:24.255
Trinh T. Minh-ha's Forgetting Vietnam

00:04:24.255 --> 00:04:27.500
and Quyen Nguyen-Le's Nuoc.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:30.730
So in terms of looking at these films,

00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:32.120
I'm looking at them through the lens

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:35.980
of trans-Vietnamese feminism
and critical refugee studies

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:38.989
to argue that water
resonates in these films

00:04:38.989 --> 00:04:42.500
as both a membrane, as a container,

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:47.500
as a bucket to use Erik's
term and as memory.

00:04:47.809 --> 00:04:50.490
And I argue that it's a conduit

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.590
for the transfer of knowledge

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:55.310
and the ocean itself becomes an archive

00:04:55.310 --> 00:05:00.050
for dead refugee bodies, but
also of stories and secrets.

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:03.340
And the films relay this
tension and poignancy,

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:06.690
both sonically and visually.

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:11.269
Such works convey waters
rehabilitative mode

00:05:11.269 --> 00:05:15.650
of tending and containing refugee grief

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:17.053
and diasporic memory.

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:24.700
So I'm gonna introduce
three modes of looking

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:29.010
that I use in order to
look at these works.

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:32.010
One of them is trans-Vietnamese feminism

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:34.860
based on the book Treacherous Subjects

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:36.854
that I'd written so long ago,

00:05:36.854 --> 00:05:39.467
but it's still relevant to me in terms

00:05:39.467 --> 00:05:43.770
of looking at the
geopolitical arrangements

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:47.090
of power in Vietnamese culture,

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:49.670
in terms of, and I'm returning

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:51.430
to Erik's terms here.

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:53.817
In terms of seeing both Vietnam

00:05:53.817 --> 00:05:58.200
and the diaspora in the same
coordinates of time and space.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.510
Ultimately, it's a method of analysis

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:04.690
that looks at these at Vietnam
and the vexed relations

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:07.620
the country shares with
its foreign others,

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:10.647
which include Western countries
like the US and France,

00:06:10.647 --> 00:06:15.130
as well as the diaspora and
its outer lying neighbors.

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:18.350
I focus on those who
have been marginalized

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:22.067
by such politics, hence the
name treacherous subjects,

00:06:22.067 --> 00:06:25.614
because these treacherous
subjects tend to be women,

00:06:25.614 --> 00:06:28.440
ethnic minorities and sexual minorities

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:31.443
or gender non-binary subjects.

00:06:33.013 --> 00:06:35.763
In terms of critical refugee studies,

00:06:36.791 --> 00:06:39.970
Yen Le Espiritu and I wrote an essay

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:42.950
on Syrian Vietnamese women artists.

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:44.410
And in this essay we talked

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:47.313
about feminist refugee
epistemology or FRE.

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:52.496
And this was an important
concept for us to push forward.

00:06:52.496 --> 00:06:57.353
And it is a way to turn away
from the spectacularization

00:06:58.195 --> 00:07:00.460
of refugee suffering that

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:02.970
often headlines global corporate media

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:07.980
to dislocate the masculinist
and nationalistic forms

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:11.810
of refugee memory that are made public

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:14.053
in local and global spaces.

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:17.650
And so as you see on the screen here,

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:20.931
we looked at Syrian borne
artist Nisrine Boukhari

00:07:20.931 --> 00:07:25.500
in a work installation
called Unreceived Letters.

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:29.170
Can you hold on for a bit,
I should close my window.

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:32.203
Can you hear the motor, lawnmower?

00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:34.460
Can anyone hear the lawnmower?

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:35.358
- [Quan] I cannot hear it, no.

00:07:35.358 --> 00:07:36.191
- [Erik] I can't hear it. It's fine.

00:07:36.191 --> 00:07:37.243
- Okay, that's great.

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:41.800
And so this is a very quiet installation

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:46.800
where Boukhari wrote letters
to her family back in Syria,

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:49.663
she's in the Netherlands right now,

00:07:49.663 --> 00:07:54.663
exiled from her own country and
she invites the participants

00:07:55.037 --> 00:07:58.490
to rifle through the letters, you know,

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:00.850
making their marks with their fingers

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:05.460
on these pieces of paper
and read through them

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:08.450
within a very private gendered space,

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:11.060
as you can tell with the little doilies.

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:16.060
And it's that intimate space
of interiority that Espiritu

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:20.533
and I wanted to emphasize.

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:25.644
On the right, you'll see
that kind of interiority

00:08:25.644 --> 00:08:29.450
in gen... My cats, quiet!

00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:33.890
And it's really a mode of
retyping, retranslating

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:38.420
some of the letters
that were sent to those

00:08:38.420 --> 00:08:43.420
who perished in the seas
during their boat passages.

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:46.910
And so the white panels
hang from the ceiling,

00:08:46.910 --> 00:08:51.910
as you can see, the white
panels being symbols of mourning

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:56.180
for those who, for those who died.

00:08:56.180 --> 00:09:01.180
And so FRE probes the
spaces of interiority

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:03.963
and the modes of silence
that in hearing them.

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:09.720
And in terms of those modes of silence,

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:12.532
it's important for us to think about

00:09:12.532 --> 00:09:17.532
what these silences
say and what they mean.

00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:22.240
How can we look at these spaces

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:27.240
and understand the silences as well

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:31.623
as the ways that as Qatari,

00:09:31.623 --> 00:09:35.407
another critical refugee
study scholar has said,

00:09:35.407 --> 00:09:38.150
"How do we understand these spaces

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:42.500
and these narratives as ways
to restory displacement."

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:47.480
And she takes the concept of
restory-ing from Chinua Achebe

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:50.398
and so a C or S methodology in short

00:09:50.398 --> 00:09:53.400
magnifies the political forces

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:56.710
that underlie these intimate sites

00:09:56.710 --> 00:09:59.120
and quite often the domestic space.

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:02.540
And I'll be using that
these kinds of lenses

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:05.830
to read the films as you'll see.

00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:10.640
Another term that I like to introduce here

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:13.440
is the idea of Refugee Cinema

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:16.910
and what Kaur and Grassilli argue

00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:20.180
is the open poethics of Fifth Cinema

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:22.810
and refugee cinema is an emergent subfield

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:25.140
within film studies.

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:28.260
And these authors argue
that refugee cinema

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:29.900
is more distinctly,

00:10:29.900 --> 00:10:34.900
a Fifth Cinema, first cinema
being what Hollywood produces.

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:40.163
Second cinema being the more
European art house films.

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:44.210
Third are the kinds of films

00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:46.538
that came out in the 1960s and seventies

00:10:46.538 --> 00:10:50.277
from Africa, Latin America, and Asia

00:10:50.277 --> 00:10:54.760
in a more mode of decolonization.

00:10:54.760 --> 00:10:59.661
So Fifth Cinema is one in
which these authors argues

00:10:59.661 --> 00:11:03.610
embodies a multifaceted spectrum of films

00:11:03.610 --> 00:11:06.890
made by or with the active
participation of refugees.

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:11.890
And quote, "its aim is
to elicit social action,"

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:14.510
and they call for a political resonant

00:11:14.510 --> 00:11:17.450
and resistant Fifth Cinema

00:11:17.450 --> 00:11:20.440
that outline the innovative often digitize

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:23.920
and dangerous means that
refugees produce their work

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:27.600
and often collaborate
with others in this mode

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:29.523
of media making.

00:11:30.580 --> 00:11:34.640
So I understand the open
poethics of Fifth Cinema,

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:37.890
and I examine these films to underscore

00:11:37.890 --> 00:11:41.180
the kind of ethical,
political and poetic...

00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:47.860
The ethics, poethics and politics

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:50.160
that underlie them as they relate

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:53.133
to refugee memories and post memories.

00:11:57.030 --> 00:12:00.480
And today I'll begin with the works

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:05.160
of Vietnamese diaspora
filmmakers chronologically.

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:07.660
And the first one that I'd like to talk

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:11.660
about is Tran Anh Hung's
La Pierre de l'Attente

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:17.880
which was done in 1991, or
it's called the Waiting Stone.

00:12:19.330 --> 00:12:24.330
And I bring this movie with
Vietnamese, Australian director,

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:28.629
Khoa Do's Mother Fish
that was made in 2009

00:12:28.629 --> 00:12:33.220
because both of these films are used

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:35.780
a certain studio setting to talk

00:12:35.780 --> 00:12:40.650
about refugee loss and grief.

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:43.230
And then in the second
part of my presentation,

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:46.410
I'm going to turn to the more recent films

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:49.113
by Trinh T. Minh-ha, Forgetting Vietnam

00:12:49.113 --> 00:12:50.990
and Quyen Nguyen-Le's Nuoc.

00:12:50.990 --> 00:12:54.150
Both of which were made in 2016

00:12:54.150 --> 00:12:57.726
to bring all of my
methodological strands together.

00:12:57.726 --> 00:13:02.726
And so to place them within
a framework of cinemas

00:13:02.860 --> 00:13:06.803
that are worth not only
looking at, but listening to.

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:13.250
So to begin Tran Anh Hung
as many of you probably know

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:17.420
is a very famous Vietnamese,
French director of films

00:13:17.420 --> 00:13:19.577
like Scent of Green Papaya, Cyclo

00:13:19.577 --> 00:13:22.750
and Vertical Ray of the Sun.

00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:27.750
And this film is actually a student film,

00:13:28.130 --> 00:13:30.600
which he made when he was a student

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:32.900
in film school in Paris.

00:13:32.900 --> 00:13:37.610
And I am attracted or drawn to this film

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:40.443
because of the ways in which
it really sets a precedent

00:13:40.443 --> 00:13:45.076
in my imagination of the kinds
of critical preoccupations

00:13:45.076 --> 00:13:50.076
about women's conditions,
about their everyday lives,

00:13:50.770 --> 00:13:55.770
the quotidian and the ways
in which aesthetically

00:13:55.890 --> 00:13:58.840
Tran Anh Hung likes to very much focus

00:14:01.100 --> 00:14:04.333
on women's faces and gestures.

00:14:05.750 --> 00:14:09.080
So these two films that I'll
be talking about back to back

00:14:11.020 --> 00:14:14.860
are held together by the
slender aesthetic thread

00:14:15.870 --> 00:14:19.810
that pulls them together
in my critical imagination.

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:24.080
And that is the unspectacular
ways that refugee memories

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:28.190
are housed in the most
minimalist of spaces.

00:14:28.190 --> 00:14:30.080
The settings for each,

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:33.420
a bleak Parisian
apartment and a sweat shop

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:36.050
that serves as a vessel for the boat,

00:14:36.050 --> 00:14:39.860
relay how refugee memories root themselves

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:44.363
in gendered spaces of
interiority and of the cycle.

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:49.400
And the reason why I place them

00:14:49.400 --> 00:14:53.204
under the ethics of refugee media making

00:14:53.204 --> 00:14:58.204
is because they are refugee
themselves, Tran Anh Hung is

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:03.370
and Khoa Do is the second generation

00:15:03.370 --> 00:15:06.900
of refugee of...

00:15:09.325 --> 00:15:13.420
He's the child of refugee
clearance in Australia,

00:15:13.420 --> 00:15:16.710
but they tried to be as
refugee-centric as possible.

00:15:16.710 --> 00:15:20.450
They wrote their own scripts
and they worked collaboratively

00:15:20.450 --> 00:15:22.760
with refugees or the children of refugees

00:15:22.760 --> 00:15:24.273
in the making of their films.

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:29.740
So here I emphasize the cinematic reframe

00:15:29.740 --> 00:15:34.740
within both of the films and
that is the absence of boats.

00:15:34.930 --> 00:15:38.600
In lacking the visualization
of boats on screen,

00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:41.320
the two directors underscore more so

00:15:41.320 --> 00:15:44.773
the oral aspects of refugee trauma.

00:15:46.266 --> 00:15:49.570
And so that in so doing,

00:15:49.570 --> 00:15:52.387
they draw meaningfully on
the connotations of water

00:15:52.387 --> 00:15:54.930
or Nuoc in Vietnamese.

00:15:54.930 --> 00:15:59.550
Nuoc as others have explored
means both water and country.

00:15:59.550 --> 00:16:02.680
But being adrift in water is a condition

00:16:02.680 --> 00:16:04.766
domain to the boat people experience

00:16:04.766 --> 00:16:07.870
in which refugees fleeing Vietnam

00:16:07.870 --> 00:16:11.709
after the Vietnam war ended in 1975,

00:16:11.709 --> 00:16:15.525
onto rickety boats, waiting for rescue

00:16:15.525 --> 00:16:20.525
is a pragmatic experience for boat people.

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:26.840
In her evocative essay, Salt/Water,

00:16:26.840 --> 00:16:29.480
Patricia Nguyen turns to performance

00:16:29.480 --> 00:16:31.520
and her family's oral histories

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:35.227
to theorize how water invokes freedom,

00:16:35.227 --> 00:16:37.450
"Freedom but also death,

00:16:37.450 --> 00:16:40.750
and is the site and
material for disrupting

00:16:40.750 --> 00:16:44.070
land-based logics of Vietnamese subject

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:45.957
formation epistemologies."

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:49.270
So like Nguyen I call attention

00:16:49.270 --> 00:16:52.070
to the ocean's harboring
of refugee subjectivities

00:16:52.908 --> 00:16:56.323
and their epistemologies.

00:16:57.730 --> 00:17:00.760
So in terms of Tran Anh Hung student film,

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:02.500
La Pierre de l'Attente,

00:17:02.500 --> 00:17:04.280
it's based on a Vietnamese folk tale

00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:07.973
called the Waiting Stone or Home Wang Fu.

00:17:08.920 --> 00:17:12.320
And it tells the story of
patriarchy's misrecognition

00:17:13.240 --> 00:17:15.300
of the incestuous other,

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:17.980
which is the sister and the construction

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:19.600
of The Waiting Woman,

00:17:19.600 --> 00:17:23.640
who's reproduced or
produced and reproduced here

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:26.510
as a result of her loyalty to her husband

00:17:27.860 --> 00:17:31.423
after she, after they find
out or the brother finds out

00:17:31.423 --> 00:17:36.423
that they are in fact, both
married and brother and sister,

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:41.520
he flees and she return,
or she stays in her home,

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:43.490
but becomes The Waiting Woman.

00:17:43.490 --> 00:17:47.270
And there's actually a rock
sculpture in Northern Vietnam of

00:17:47.270 --> 00:17:51.410
The Waiting Woman where she
is petrified into stone,

00:17:51.410 --> 00:17:55.253
the figure is, while
she's holding her son.

00:17:56.360 --> 00:18:00.140
So this folk tale in the filming version

00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:04.390
is reused interestingly,

00:18:04.390 --> 00:18:08.860
in ways that reflect on the
Vietnamese refugee experience.

00:18:08.860 --> 00:18:12.460
And it updates the legend to
a more contemporary setting

00:18:12.460 --> 00:18:16.650
where the characters arrive
at a refugee camp in Indonesia

00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:19.908
and are then relocated to France.

00:18:19.908 --> 00:18:20.751
And in my mind,

00:18:20.751 --> 00:18:23.508
the move just speaks an innovative

00:18:23.508 --> 00:18:26.550
and creative use of a studio setting

00:18:26.550 --> 00:18:30.090
where the refugee camp
set and the drab interiors

00:18:30.090 --> 00:18:32.610
of an apartment in Paris.

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:37.170
So we shift from the camp's
barracks to the blocking lines

00:18:37.170 --> 00:18:38.830
of a gray building.

00:18:38.830 --> 00:18:41.903
And the two set pieces
are highly suggestive

00:18:41.903 --> 00:18:44.570
of the ways that refugee lives appear

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:48.000
to involve endless forms of waiting,

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:51.039
as they're processed, as
refugees at the camps,

00:18:51.039 --> 00:18:55.420
and then become immigrants
in their new environments.

00:18:55.420 --> 00:18:58.003
Waiting as it were to begin again.

00:19:02.320 --> 00:19:07.290
So I wanted to begin
here with a short clip

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:14.000
of this moment where we're in the camps

00:19:14.290 --> 00:19:16.157
and we have Tran Nu Yen Khe

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:23.210
who is Tran Anh Hung's partner and wife

00:19:23.210 --> 00:19:28.210
in an earlier film of his who
plays the main protagonist.

00:19:29.678 --> 00:19:34.093
And then we also have
LumLe who is a director,

00:19:34.093 --> 00:19:38.430
a Vietnamese, French director
himself who plays the brother.

00:19:38.430 --> 00:19:41.020
And in this introduction,

00:19:41.020 --> 00:19:43.990
I wanted to have you
think about the ways that,

00:19:43.990 --> 00:19:47.800
again, we're in, we're
set in a studio setting,

00:19:47.800 --> 00:19:52.643
which has been redone
as the camps barracks.

00:19:53.834 --> 00:19:56.834
(people chattering)

00:19:59.354 --> 00:20:01.854
(baby crying)

00:20:03.329 --> 00:20:06.773
(women laughing)

00:20:06.773 --> 00:20:09.606
(foghorn blaring)

00:20:14.702 --> 00:20:17.702
(people chattering)

00:20:33.509 --> 00:20:37.592
- (speaking in foreign language)

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:48.113
Okay.

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:54.890
The only clip that I could
get was actually on YouTube

00:20:54.890 --> 00:20:57.640
and with Spanish subtitles.

00:20:57.640 --> 00:21:01.100
Otherwise the film is only
available from what I can tell

00:21:01.100 --> 00:21:04.891
in a space called Cinematheque in Paris.

00:21:04.891 --> 00:21:08.493
And that's how I got access to the film.

00:21:09.930 --> 00:21:14.930
But so apologies in
advance for the subtitles,

00:21:15.440 --> 00:21:19.475
this introduction though says
a lot through the gestural

00:21:19.475 --> 00:21:24.475
and through the use of
light and the sonic tapestry

00:21:25.120 --> 00:21:28.640
that I wanted to have you think about.

00:21:28.640 --> 00:21:32.470
So this young woman
levers us into the story

00:21:32.470 --> 00:21:34.620
when she enters the film frames

00:21:34.620 --> 00:21:37.520
and steps into the refugee camp,

00:21:37.520 --> 00:21:40.890
generically named as such
in the French subtitles.

00:21:40.890 --> 00:21:44.347
Amid children playing and
men and women talking,

00:21:44.347 --> 00:21:47.990
the sounds in the camp
signal a hum of activities.

00:21:47.990 --> 00:21:52.923
In the background you also
hear ocean waves and foghorns,

00:21:52.923 --> 00:21:55.950
and instead of a fishing
village, as you see,

00:21:55.950 --> 00:21:58.080
or as you know,

00:21:58.080 --> 00:22:03.080
from the Vietnamese legend,
it's what's cited here

00:22:03.350 --> 00:22:06.930
is the camp itself where the siblings bond

00:22:06.930 --> 00:22:08.360
as a direct result of having

00:22:08.360 --> 00:22:11.610
survived the boat passage alone.

00:22:11.610 --> 00:22:13.630
During their spare dialogue,

00:22:13.630 --> 00:22:17.400
the hues of the sequence
evoke a semblance of warmth,

00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:20.860
even as it's set in the
bareness of the barracks.

00:22:20.860 --> 00:22:23.890
So I note the sound color
and tone of the scene

00:22:23.890 --> 00:22:26.400
because they contrast dramatically

00:22:26.400 --> 00:22:28.630
with the following scenes,

00:22:28.630 --> 00:22:31.490
where the couple now husband and wife

00:22:31.490 --> 00:22:33.940
live in a Parisian apartment complex

00:22:33.940 --> 00:22:37.010
that is dreary and devoid of sound.

00:22:37.010 --> 00:22:38.703
So with this change of scenery,

00:22:39.800 --> 00:22:43.800
we move from the warm sounds
and palette of the ocean

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:46.860
and the camp to a soundless new year

00:22:46.860 --> 00:22:50.463
and the muted colors of
their resettlement in France.

00:22:51.810 --> 00:22:54.490
So in accommodating the myth to the story

00:22:54.490 --> 00:22:58.930
of the Vietnamese displaced,
Jun reimagines of folktale

00:22:58.930 --> 00:23:03.300
to tell a bleak story of
loss and wars after life.

00:23:03.300 --> 00:23:05.780
And this reframing further locates,

00:23:05.780 --> 00:23:08.700
the brother and the husband and the sister

00:23:08.700 --> 00:23:11.130
and the wife's estrangement

00:23:11.130 --> 00:23:14.713
within the confines of
their new surroundings.

00:23:15.970 --> 00:23:20.440
So that while the film
sounds out a confucianus tale

00:23:20.440 --> 00:23:22.440
about women's endurance,

00:23:22.440 --> 00:23:26.440
the familial story that Jun retells

00:23:26.440 --> 00:23:30.470
is lined with refugee desires to leave

00:23:30.470 --> 00:23:34.543
and fears in both their
host and home countries.

00:23:35.970 --> 00:23:40.970
And the devastation of the
film then is internally tied

00:23:41.130 --> 00:23:44.950
to Vietnamese refugees and
their transnational journeys

00:23:44.950 --> 00:23:47.959
and the material and existential losses

00:23:47.959 --> 00:23:50.993
that they have incurred along the way.

00:23:55.020 --> 00:24:00.020
So what I argue is that the
film emphasizes a sense of exile

00:24:00.421 --> 00:24:04.930
and grief that are restory'd here in a way

00:24:04.930 --> 00:24:07.940
that speaks over the moralistic

00:24:07.940 --> 00:24:11.090
and patriarchal overtones of the legend.

00:24:11.090 --> 00:24:13.170
And the director adjust us instead

00:24:13.170 --> 00:24:16.280
to how refugees endeavor
to not only survive

00:24:16.280 --> 00:24:18.270
the oceanic passage,

00:24:18.270 --> 00:24:22.233
but also survive the
resettlement experience as well.

00:24:23.440 --> 00:24:25.237
I'm gonna move on to mother...

00:24:25.237 --> 00:24:27.620
(people chattering)

00:24:27.620 --> 00:24:31.580
Mother Fish by
Vietnamese-Australian, Khoa Do.

00:24:31.580 --> 00:24:34.619
And so like Tran Anh Hung's La Pierre,

00:24:34.619 --> 00:24:38.600
the film begins with a
black screen replaying

00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:41.050
in the backdrop voices that are familiar

00:24:41.050 --> 00:24:43.017
to an Australian public.

00:24:43.017 --> 00:24:46.560
Former Australian prime
minister, Kevin Rudd

00:24:46.560 --> 00:24:50.430
discusses the draconian
raft of refugee policies

00:24:50.430 --> 00:24:53.240
called the Pacific Solution.

00:24:53.240 --> 00:24:58.240
So you hear in the background
while a black screen plays

00:24:58.550 --> 00:25:03.480
of a reporter talking or
asking Prime Minister Rudd

00:25:03.480 --> 00:25:07.810
on the possibility of
reopening Christmas island

00:25:07.810 --> 00:25:11.074
as a site to process asylum planes.

00:25:11.074 --> 00:25:13.270
And Rudd vaguely responds,

00:25:13.270 --> 00:25:17.970
there's a purpose-built
facility there for that purpose.

00:25:17.970 --> 00:25:19.823
So following this empty rhetoric,

00:25:19.823 --> 00:25:23.390
that there's a sound
bridge of running water

00:25:23.390 --> 00:25:25.037
that leads us into the kitchen

00:25:25.037 --> 00:25:29.653
where a Vietnamese woman
soundlessly prepares a meal.

00:25:30.670 --> 00:25:31.630
So from here,

00:25:31.630 --> 00:25:35.216
we moved to a factory setting
where the refugee passage

00:25:35.216 --> 00:25:39.430
is again, reenacted and reimagined.

00:25:39.430 --> 00:25:42.133
So I wanted to play a quick clip of that,

00:25:44.850 --> 00:25:47.808
of the films beginning.

00:25:47.808 --> 00:25:50.135
- [Male Voiceover] Take people
when they're intercepted

00:25:50.135 --> 00:25:52.884
in Australian waters,
that's where they go.

00:25:52.884 --> 00:25:55.470
Secondly, there's a
purposeful facility there

00:25:55.470 --> 00:25:57.020
for that purpose.

00:25:57.020 --> 00:25:59.521
And as far as I know,
and no recent advices,

00:25:59.521 --> 00:26:01.256
there is still a capacity there.

00:26:01.256 --> 00:26:03.774
But as the ministers made
claims for a long, long time.

00:26:03.774 --> 00:26:05.038
- [Female Voiceover]
(speaking in foreign language)

00:26:05.038 --> 00:26:06.810
- [Male Voiceover] Of course
had additional capacity

00:26:06.810 --> 00:26:09.590
of Darwin and other places
should that become necessary.

00:26:09.590 --> 00:26:11.243
What we've dealt with in the last-

00:26:11.243 --> 00:26:13.216
- [Female Voiceover]
(speaking in foreign language)

00:26:13.216 --> 00:26:14.512
- [Male Voiceover] Is the-

00:26:14.512 --> 00:26:18.019
- [Female Voiceover]
(speaking in foreign language)

00:26:18.019 --> 00:26:19.340
- [Male Voiceover] A
civil war in Sri Lanka-

00:26:19.340 --> 00:26:22.431
- [Female Voiceover]
(speaking in foreign language)

00:26:22.431 --> 00:26:23.372
- [Male Voiceover] Across the world.

00:26:23.372 --> 00:26:25.568
- Okay, so in that quick clip,

00:26:25.568 --> 00:26:29.650
you hear Rudd's voice in a,

00:26:29.650 --> 00:26:33.470
in a voiceover that is also
intermingled with the sounds of

00:26:33.470 --> 00:26:37.868
cooking and it's within
these unspectacular confines,

00:26:37.868 --> 00:26:41.744
that the story of Vietnamese
boat people really emerges.

00:26:41.744 --> 00:26:44.180
And while she's preparing her food,

00:26:44.180 --> 00:26:47.940
the woman hears another
woman's voice as you heard,

00:26:47.940 --> 00:26:51.120
which turns out to be
her younger sister Han.

00:26:51.120 --> 00:26:54.980
Speaking to her playfully
in an interior monologue

00:26:54.980 --> 00:26:57.930
that plays out in the
beginning and end of the film,

00:26:57.930 --> 00:27:01.020
but what also begins
and ends with the film,

00:27:01.020 --> 00:27:03.840
but what also begins and ends the film

00:27:03.840 --> 00:27:06.670
are the sounds of water

00:27:06.670 --> 00:27:10.450
and the two sisters reunite in water.

00:27:10.450 --> 00:27:12.708
Actually they have submerged in water

00:27:12.708 --> 00:27:16.853
and it's a coming together
that we actually see on screen.

00:27:18.010 --> 00:27:20.710
But before continuing with Kim' story,

00:27:20.710 --> 00:27:24.660
who is the adult Kim that
you see cooking here,

00:27:24.660 --> 00:27:25.493
I'd like to offer some notes
on the Pacific Solution

00:27:25.493 --> 00:27:30.258
and its devastating impacts on
refugees and asylum seekers.

00:27:32.300 --> 00:27:36.900
Established in 2001 and
promulgated by the red government.

00:27:36.900 --> 00:27:40.530
The Pacific Solution was
designed to refuse refugees

00:27:40.530 --> 00:27:42.640
on the grounds that Australia needed

00:27:42.640 --> 00:27:47.050
to defend its borders against boat people

00:27:47.050 --> 00:27:49.618
who were unlawfully entering the country,

00:27:49.618 --> 00:27:52.370
arriving on its shores.

00:27:52.370 --> 00:27:53.790
And part of the solution

00:27:53.790 --> 00:27:56.390
was the rezoning of Christmas island.

00:27:56.390 --> 00:27:59.420
Which would no longer
receive asylum seekers

00:27:59.420 --> 00:28:01.630
and refugees.

00:28:01.630 --> 00:28:03.470
Instead, the Australian government

00:28:03.470 --> 00:28:08.280
set up offshore reception
centers in Papua New Guinea

00:28:08.280 --> 00:28:13.280
and Nora to outsource the
work of processing refugees.

00:28:13.500 --> 00:28:16.060
In 2013 under Rudd's direction

00:28:16.060 --> 00:28:18.720
in his second term as prime minister,

00:28:18.720 --> 00:28:22.620
a revised version of the
Pacific Solution was rolled out.

00:28:22.620 --> 00:28:24.360
In which this newest iteration

00:28:24.360 --> 00:28:28.111
of this law formally enacts
the physical detention

00:28:28.111 --> 00:28:32.400
of refugees and the
reshuffling of their borders,

00:28:32.400 --> 00:28:36.630
of their bodies to any
other place, but Australia.

00:28:36.630 --> 00:28:40.000
And of course the precursor
to these solutions

00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:43.549
was the problem that Indo-Chinese refugees

00:28:43.549 --> 00:28:48.000
as both people had posed when
they first came to Australia

00:28:48.000 --> 00:28:51.230
in the 1970s up to the 1980s.

00:28:51.230 --> 00:28:53.590
At the time much of
Australian conservative

00:28:53.590 --> 00:28:57.200
political discourse
positioned the end of Chinese

00:28:57.200 --> 00:29:01.410
as imposters and not bonafide refugees.

00:29:01.410 --> 00:29:04.530
Understood as having
left Vietnam by choice

00:29:04.530 --> 00:29:07.090
and out of economic need.

00:29:07.090 --> 00:29:09.890
So with these overlapping
historical contexts

00:29:09.890 --> 00:29:11.370
that begin the film,

00:29:11.370 --> 00:29:14.930
Do's work insists on restoring

00:29:14.930 --> 00:29:17.280
the narratives of Vietnamese boat people

00:29:18.210 --> 00:29:22.760
in speaking to the
Vietnamese-Australian refugee community

00:29:22.760 --> 00:29:27.670
and other refugee irregular
maritime migrants,

00:29:27.670 --> 00:29:31.180
as they're called in Australian media,

00:29:31.180 --> 00:29:35.650
who have been refused
recently by the government.

00:29:35.650 --> 00:29:40.140
And these connective
tissues between boat people

00:29:40.140 --> 00:29:45.140
and recent refugees book in the film.

00:29:45.390 --> 00:29:48.910
And it begins with Australia's
xenophobic political

00:29:48.910 --> 00:29:51.700
discourse and ends on a commemoration

00:29:51.700 --> 00:29:53.350
of Vietnamese boat people.

00:29:53.350 --> 00:29:55.719
The closing intertitles reveal that,

00:29:55.719 --> 00:30:00.719
"around 600,000 of an estimated
1.5 million Vietnamese

00:30:00.999 --> 00:30:03.170
who fled their country by boat

00:30:03.170 --> 00:30:08.170
between 1975 and 1996 perished at sea."

00:30:11.300 --> 00:30:12.700
So with this next clip,

00:30:12.700 --> 00:30:17.700
I wanted to show how he,
Khoa Do actually visualizes

00:30:18.540 --> 00:30:23.540
this boat passage within
a garment factory space.

00:30:24.370 --> 00:30:26.490
And it's what once very beautiful

00:30:26.490 --> 00:30:31.490
and poignant and other times,
very derealized formally,

00:30:33.580 --> 00:30:35.766
because one has to fill in the spaces

00:30:35.766 --> 00:30:38.513
of the space of the boat.

00:30:39.500 --> 00:30:43.123
And so here's a quick
clip to exemplify that.

00:30:44.449 --> 00:30:48.532
- (speaking in foreign language)

00:30:53.239 --> 00:30:56.156
(thunder rumbling)

00:31:31.660 --> 00:31:34.243
- I'm gonna get some more food.

00:31:37.867 --> 00:31:38.700
- Food.

00:31:45.283 --> 00:31:48.033
- How long you been out here for?

00:31:49.553 --> 00:31:50.720
- Three weeks.

00:31:53.040 --> 00:31:57.310
- So I'll stop there, but
you can see the steeples of,

00:31:57.310 --> 00:32:01.790
you know, the red cones
of thread in the back.

00:32:01.790 --> 00:32:05.920
You see this kind of rusted
manufacturing equipment

00:32:05.920 --> 00:32:10.920
that offer viewers an
imaginative invitation

00:32:11.070 --> 00:32:13.753
to see that this is the pull of the boat.

00:32:15.160 --> 00:32:18.240
And so in so doing, it forces the viewer

00:32:18.240 --> 00:32:21.510
to imagine the imaginable, unimaginable

00:32:21.510 --> 00:32:24.154
with sounds and images of water

00:32:24.154 --> 00:32:27.480
as pneumonic devices that allow the viewer

00:32:27.480 --> 00:32:30.980
to be wrapped up in, or I'm sorry,

00:32:30.980 --> 00:32:34.170
wrapped up in the emotional
tides of the film.

00:32:34.170 --> 00:32:35.430
So as it plays out,

00:32:35.430 --> 00:32:38.910
as you see the main protagonists here

00:32:38.910 --> 00:32:40.380
are the two sisters

00:32:40.380 --> 00:32:44.700
and the one of the sisters do not survive.

00:32:44.700 --> 00:32:47.490
So my dual focus in terms of looking

00:32:47.490 --> 00:32:52.230
at La Pierre de l'Attente and Mother Fish,

00:32:52.230 --> 00:32:56.300
is to look at both the
setting and the sound.

00:32:56.300 --> 00:33:01.000
Both the familiarity of these
stories in their formalism

00:33:01.000 --> 00:33:05.400
as a way to alienate in (indistinct) way,

00:33:05.400 --> 00:33:08.360
the viewer from the surroundings.

00:33:08.360 --> 00:33:10.050
They are, all of these elements

00:33:10.050 --> 00:33:11.440
are part of the criticality

00:33:11.440 --> 00:33:15.270
that I believe Tran Anh
Hung and Khoa Do wields,

00:33:15.270 --> 00:33:19.345
wield in producing refugee centered films

00:33:19.345 --> 00:33:24.063
that in turn speak to and about refugees.

00:33:28.050 --> 00:33:31.450
Which I think leads us
to the other two films

00:33:31.450 --> 00:33:33.980
that I'd like to speak about

00:33:33.980 --> 00:33:36.124
within the first two set of films,

00:33:36.124 --> 00:33:41.124
we are very much in a private space

00:33:41.292 --> 00:33:44.920
and I think Forgetting Vietnam and Nuoc

00:33:44.920 --> 00:33:48.770
are also two films that talk about water

00:33:48.770 --> 00:33:53.770
and sea, in ways that
more obviously to me,

00:33:56.330 --> 00:33:58.240
or more apparently to me

00:33:58.240 --> 00:34:02.830
through what I call is a
trans-Vietnamese feminist lens.

00:34:02.830 --> 00:34:06.790
So I want to talk about
in the next portion,

00:34:06.790 --> 00:34:11.590
in this presentation about
the caring for the living

00:34:11.590 --> 00:34:12.660
and for the dead.

00:34:12.660 --> 00:34:16.640
And it's a trip that I find
as a critical refugee scholar,

00:34:16.640 --> 00:34:20.800
very compelling in reading
the cultural productions

00:34:20.800 --> 00:34:22.633
of the Vietnamese diaspora.

00:34:24.260 --> 00:34:28.400
Refugee memory and critical inquiry

00:34:28.400 --> 00:34:31.600
are what I see as being centralized

00:34:31.600 --> 00:34:34.670
in these two films in such ways

00:34:34.670 --> 00:34:39.670
that these experimental works
by women and queer filmmakers,

00:34:39.750 --> 00:34:44.070
expound on water, silence, and intimacy,

00:34:44.070 --> 00:34:48.500
but also the open, breaks
open the possibility

00:34:48.500 --> 00:34:52.950
of critiquing the state and allowing

00:34:52.950 --> 00:34:56.703
for a queer sociality within these spaces.

00:34:57.990 --> 00:35:00.840
So, as I argue for both of the films,

00:35:00.840 --> 00:35:04.920
water is especially
incubative and maternal,

00:35:04.920 --> 00:35:07.327
the source of life giving properties,

00:35:07.327 --> 00:35:11.140
as well as the holder of secrets

00:35:11.140 --> 00:35:12.763
against the Vietnamese state.

00:35:13.890 --> 00:35:18.453
And I'll be talking about,
Forgetting Vietnam first,

00:35:21.050 --> 00:35:23.190
it's dedicated to the memories of those

00:35:23.190 --> 00:35:25.120
who survived the war.

00:35:25.120 --> 00:35:29.014
And the film forges then
a critique of the state,

00:35:29.014 --> 00:35:32.649
and is really lined with
memories of the hereness

00:35:32.649 --> 00:35:36.945
of the country, which is
captured on digital video,

00:35:36.945 --> 00:35:40.020
but also the absent bodies of those

00:35:40.020 --> 00:35:43.083
who did not survive the boat passage.

00:35:44.090 --> 00:35:48.760
Whose bodies are not actually
represented on screen either.

00:35:48.760 --> 00:35:52.230
And my argument is that
you hear these stories

00:35:52.230 --> 00:35:55.100
and you see the images
and you hear the lapping

00:35:55.100 --> 00:35:57.110
of the waves and the, again,

00:35:57.110 --> 00:36:02.110
those devices are pneumonic devices

00:36:03.340 --> 00:36:08.173
for us to remember and
reimagine those dead bodies.

00:36:09.170 --> 00:36:11.690
So Forgetting Vietnam, archives personal

00:36:11.690 --> 00:36:16.080
and collective memory
by way of a travel log.

00:36:16.080 --> 00:36:19.280
Touring Vietnam in this
way is important for me

00:36:19.280 --> 00:36:23.168
in just seeing how a diasporic identity

00:36:23.168 --> 00:36:27.230
through Trinh T. Minh-ha's
perspective is a sieve

00:36:27.230 --> 00:36:30.860
through which the works
affects are filtered.

00:36:30.860 --> 00:36:34.380
And it's marked by the state,
this diasporic perspective

00:36:34.380 --> 00:36:37.020
is marked by the state as an outsider,

00:36:37.020 --> 00:36:39.348
a former trader to the nation.

00:36:39.348 --> 00:36:44.348
But with this kind of subjectivity,

00:36:45.000 --> 00:36:48.630
this Vietnamese diasporic
subject is allowed,

00:36:48.630 --> 00:36:52.253
I think a way in to critique the state.

00:36:54.660 --> 00:36:59.200
Forgetting Vietnam is critical
of the state's ongoing

00:36:59.200 --> 00:37:03.390
operations to forget and
erase the histories of those

00:37:03.390 --> 00:37:05.010
who have survived the war,

00:37:05.010 --> 00:37:07.720
doing so by representing war again

00:37:07.720 --> 00:37:10.263
as the keeper of secrets and knowledge.

00:37:11.250 --> 00:37:13.320
And in this short clip,

00:37:13.320 --> 00:37:18.320
we have a Vietnamese bus driver
talking about these secrets.

00:37:18.810 --> 00:37:21.880
And one of the things
that Trinh T. Minh-ha does

00:37:21.880 --> 00:37:24.878
throughout the film actually is to conjoin

00:37:24.878 --> 00:37:29.878
the idea that Nuoc and Dat, or Dat Nuoc

00:37:30.370 --> 00:37:34.400
are conjoined together in Vietnamese

00:37:34.400 --> 00:37:37.133
and Vietnamese diasporic memories.

00:37:38.220 --> 00:37:41.430
And so while we're, he's
talking the bus driver,

00:37:41.430 --> 00:37:45.500
we see expanses and expanses of land,

00:37:45.500 --> 00:37:48.010
most notably of rice fields.

00:37:48.010 --> 00:37:51.480
Later on the film has a lot of shots

00:37:51.480 --> 00:37:53.960
of moving waters as well.

00:37:53.960 --> 00:37:58.400
But in this clip, we hear
then the ways in which the,

00:37:58.400 --> 00:38:00.940
the people, working
class people of Vietnam

00:38:00.940 --> 00:38:03.663
are often very critical of the state.

00:38:05.616 --> 00:38:10.616
- [Male Voiceover] (speaking
in foreign language)

00:38:23.419 --> 00:38:24.253
- [Female Voiceover]
(speaking in foreign language)

00:38:24.253 --> 00:38:29.253
[Male Voiceover] (speaking
in foreign language)

00:38:36.158 --> 00:38:38.741
(man laughing)

00:38:42.535 --> 00:38:45.118
(cock crowing)

00:38:46.800 --> 00:38:50.020
So Forgetting Vietnam addresses
how both land and water

00:38:50.020 --> 00:38:51.649
as that Dat Nuoc

00:38:51.649 --> 00:38:55.920
constitute the country's
complex memory scape,

00:38:55.920 --> 00:38:59.540
signaled by these images
of land and water,

00:38:59.540 --> 00:39:03.519
the film most insistingly cites the sea as

00:39:03.519 --> 00:39:06.852
(speaks in Vietnamese).

00:39:07.756 --> 00:39:10.753
As more amniotic than traumatic.

00:39:14.322 --> 00:39:16.540
In Trinh's Forgetting Vietnam

00:39:16.540 --> 00:39:18.650
makes a case then for the ways that water

00:39:18.650 --> 00:39:21.960
stores knowledge and
retains memories of those

00:39:21.960 --> 00:39:24.610
that had been disappeared by their state.

00:39:24.610 --> 00:39:27.313
A notion reinforced by the fact that Nuoc,

00:39:27.313 --> 00:39:32.010
as the film notes early on
means both water and country

00:39:32.010 --> 00:39:35.701
and invokes the origin story of

00:39:35.701 --> 00:39:38.730
(speaks in foreign language).

00:39:38.730 --> 00:39:40.870
Whereby the separation of the people

00:39:40.870 --> 00:39:44.390
has been born out of loss and love.

00:39:44.390 --> 00:39:47.928
Forgetting rehearses the
country's original trauma

00:39:47.928 --> 00:39:52.810
of separation to reflect
then upon the divisions

00:39:52.810 --> 00:39:57.810
that have also striated
Vietnam's past and present,

00:39:58.120 --> 00:40:01.310
the painful divisions that
have marked north and south,

00:40:01.310 --> 00:40:05.173
as well as the divisions between
Vietnam and the diaspora.

00:40:06.758 --> 00:40:09.790
I'm gonna move on to
Quyen Nguyen-Le's Nuoc

00:40:11.030 --> 00:40:14.740
because if Trinh T. Minh-ha
filmed dwells on Dat Nuoc

00:40:14.740 --> 00:40:19.320
to speak of Vietnam and
warn against its forgetting

00:40:19.320 --> 00:40:21.080
of refugee lives.

00:40:21.080 --> 00:40:24.290
Le's 6-minute short Nuoc concretizes

00:40:24.290 --> 00:40:26.720
the imagery of Vietnamese land,

00:40:26.720 --> 00:40:30.268
specifically its production
of rice and water

00:40:30.268 --> 00:40:33.993
through the evocations
of food and food making.

00:40:33.993 --> 00:40:38.210
So that what runs through
the film are acts of making

00:40:38.210 --> 00:40:41.210
and eating food specifically chao

00:40:41.210 --> 00:40:43.674
or rice porridge, which is constituted

00:40:43.674 --> 00:40:46.920
by both rice and water.

00:40:46.920 --> 00:40:49.920
In fact, liquids run as a current

00:40:49.920 --> 00:40:53.195
from beginning to end were first situated

00:40:53.195 --> 00:40:56.560
in a photography dark room where chemicals

00:40:56.560 --> 00:41:00.140
and water are used to
create memory and culture.

00:41:00.140 --> 00:41:03.958
Most specifically of photographs of family

00:41:03.958 --> 00:41:05.543
and of the war.

00:41:07.010 --> 00:41:10.070
And the associations between
the gender queer subject

00:41:10.070 --> 00:41:11.760
that is at the heart of the film

00:41:11.760 --> 00:41:16.760
and the mother become more
clarified as the film continues.

00:41:17.380 --> 00:41:21.400
And what we have on
this image that you see

00:41:21.400 --> 00:41:25.700
is that Qhua the mother
and Rosie eat chao.

00:41:25.700 --> 00:41:29.323
The scenes are composed
of a concise study,

00:41:30.570 --> 00:41:33.378
they compose a concise study in the ways

00:41:33.378 --> 00:41:36.414
that the two subjects are often nourished,

00:41:36.414 --> 00:41:38.790
not only through daily food,

00:41:38.790 --> 00:41:42.810
but also through the vital
stories they tell one another.

00:41:42.810 --> 00:41:47.650
The emphasis on these
unspectacular refugee acts,

00:41:47.650 --> 00:41:51.850
as they're recreated in
refugee cultural productions is

00:41:51.850 --> 00:41:54.960
critical as Espiritu and I have argued.

00:41:54.960 --> 00:41:56.910
The every day and the intimacies

00:41:56.910 --> 00:42:00.480
between diasporic subjects
are the foundations

00:42:00.480 --> 00:42:05.040
for what we argue are feminist alliances

00:42:05.040 --> 00:42:06.740
and refugee knowledges.

00:42:08.230 --> 00:42:11.110
A feminist refugee epistemology appears

00:42:11.110 --> 00:42:13.220
in the everyday objects

00:42:13.220 --> 00:42:17.090
within a context of
silence and quiet tune.

00:42:17.090 --> 00:42:20.003
And allows us to see the feminist

00:42:20.003 --> 00:42:24.360
and queer visual practices
by which refugee artists

00:42:24.360 --> 00:42:29.360
reclaim the private space as
a gendered and queered space,

00:42:29.810 --> 00:42:32.560
expressive of loss and grief,

00:42:32.560 --> 00:42:35.310
but also of creative improvised

00:42:35.310 --> 00:42:39.660
and experimental refugee making practices.

00:42:39.660 --> 00:42:41.973
So here, as you'll see in the clip,

00:42:43.456 --> 00:42:47.090
there is a primal
relationship between mother

00:42:47.090 --> 00:42:50.000
and gender queer subject that constitutes

00:42:50.000 --> 00:42:53.500
the film's main affective artery.

00:42:53.500 --> 00:42:55.720
Just to give you some context,

00:42:55.720 --> 00:43:00.720
what you see is Rosy here
in performing a kind of

00:43:02.210 --> 00:43:07.103
gestational labor, pulling
out a bloody umbilical cord.

00:43:10.532 --> 00:43:14.440
And then there's a very
quick juxtaposition

00:43:14.440 --> 00:43:18.230
to another scene of Rosie being pulled out

00:43:18.230 --> 00:43:21.804
of a boat by her mother, by their mother.

00:43:21.804 --> 00:43:24.387
(upbeat music)

00:43:31.529 --> 00:43:34.529
(suspenseful music)

00:43:42.887 --> 00:43:45.637
(birds chirping)

00:43:49.065 --> 00:43:51.648
(rain tapping)

00:43:57.450 --> 00:44:01.533
- (speaking in foreign language)

00:44:05.221 --> 00:44:07.721
- I think she'd like that too.

00:44:12.216 --> 00:44:14.799
(gentle music)

00:44:19.914 --> 00:44:21.247
- So as earlier,

00:44:22.300 --> 00:44:26.600
you saw a painful scene
of gestation and labor

00:44:26.600 --> 00:44:29.870
whereby Rosie tries to get the cord out,

00:44:29.870 --> 00:44:34.060
but with the same kind of
pulling gesture in the next shot,

00:44:34.060 --> 00:44:37.243
we see the mother pulling
Rosie out of water

00:44:37.243 --> 00:44:40.490
onto a boat in effect saving their life

00:44:40.490 --> 00:44:43.120
and steering them towards safety.

00:44:43.120 --> 00:44:48.120
So water here is a life-giving power

00:44:48.270 --> 00:44:50.720
that's vested in the maternal figure.

00:44:50.720 --> 00:44:54.300
And it illuminates on the
grief that is inherited

00:44:54.300 --> 00:44:56.270
between the generations,

00:44:56.270 --> 00:44:59.260
between those subjects
who have survived the war,

00:44:59.260 --> 00:45:03.009
as seen in the figure of Qhua the mother,

00:45:03.009 --> 00:45:06.850
and those who have survived its aftermath

00:45:06.850 --> 00:45:11.850
those who are like Rosie as
a second generation subject.

00:45:13.900 --> 00:45:17.660
In the metaphorical and
affective registers of the film

00:45:17.660 --> 00:45:20.860
fluidity between these temporalities,

00:45:20.860 --> 00:45:25.290
spatial geographies, genders
as well as generations

00:45:25.290 --> 00:45:27.410
is symbolized by water.

00:45:27.410 --> 00:45:31.490
Water is this liquid
encasement that fuses together

00:45:31.490 --> 00:45:36.490
mother with motherland, life
with a queer feminist lineage

00:45:36.680 --> 00:45:39.259
and that which binds the diasporic subject

00:45:39.259 --> 00:45:42.900
to the motherland or Nuoc.

00:45:44.060 --> 00:45:47.390
So that it's appropriate
that in the concluding scene

00:45:47.390 --> 00:45:50.010
of the film, it features rain

00:45:50.010 --> 00:45:52.700
as the iterative act of cleansing

00:45:52.700 --> 00:45:55.010
and renewal and it demonstrates

00:45:55.010 --> 00:45:59.520
how the two protagonists come
together surrounded by water.

00:45:59.520 --> 00:46:02.990
Their mundane act of eating, sharing food,

00:46:02.990 --> 00:46:06.220
and silence becomes an
opening between them,

00:46:06.220 --> 00:46:09.380
a revolutionary act of making space

00:46:09.380 --> 00:46:12.101
for queerness within the home.

00:46:12.101 --> 00:46:15.620
As seen in the last line of dialogue,

00:46:15.620 --> 00:46:19.560
the mother invites Rosie's
friend to eat in their home

00:46:19.560 --> 00:46:21.340
to eat chao.

00:46:21.340 --> 00:46:25.690
In Nuoc, the complicated labors
that the diasporic subject

00:46:25.690 --> 00:46:30.690
has to navigate is affected by
way of a meaningful dialogue,

00:46:31.530 --> 00:46:36.233
a dialectical relationship with
both mother and mother land.

00:46:39.630 --> 00:46:44.520
So in conclusion, in the
films that I've analyzed here

00:46:44.520 --> 00:46:48.220
from La Pierre de
l'Attente to Mother Fish,

00:46:48.220 --> 00:46:52.720
Forgetting Vietnam to Nuoc,
water archives the lives

00:46:52.720 --> 00:46:55.900
and memories of the Vietnamese diaspora.

00:46:55.900 --> 00:46:59.895
Some of whom work to
illuminate on the refugee hoods

00:46:59.895 --> 00:47:04.895
that have lined their family
histories and their memories.

00:47:05.829 --> 00:47:07.060
And in closing,

00:47:07.060 --> 00:47:09.470
I end here on a shot
from Trinh T. Minh-ha's

00:47:09.470 --> 00:47:13.320
Forgetting Vietnam and I contend here

00:47:13.320 --> 00:47:16.535
and elsewhere for the
critical modes of diasporic

00:47:16.535 --> 00:47:21.080
and refugee ways of knowing
that occur in silence,

00:47:21.080 --> 00:47:25.110
speech, in images of art and film

00:47:25.110 --> 00:47:28.203
in terms of water and ocean.

00:47:29.320 --> 00:47:34.180
And I conjoin the study of
diasporic refugee cinema

00:47:34.180 --> 00:47:36.420
with a focus on water,

00:47:36.420 --> 00:47:39.110
to gesture towards country, sustenance

00:47:39.110 --> 00:47:41.193
and the maternal at once.

00:47:42.250 --> 00:47:44.252
And I conjoin the study of cinema

00:47:44.252 --> 00:47:46.940
with critical refugee studies,

00:47:46.940 --> 00:47:50.403
as well as trans-Vietnamese feminism.

00:47:52.480 --> 00:47:53.793
In conclusion,

00:47:55.000 --> 00:47:58.480
I'd like to emphasize that
the waters of the ocean

00:47:58.480 --> 00:48:00.900
as framed in these four films,

00:48:00.900 --> 00:48:03.460
as heard in these four films,

00:48:03.460 --> 00:48:06.360
in its beauty and volatility

00:48:06.360 --> 00:48:08.800
has contained the bodies of refugees,

00:48:08.800 --> 00:48:12.270
but also holds in its expansive folds,

00:48:12.270 --> 00:48:14.960
the possibility of knowledge.

00:48:14.960 --> 00:48:19.771
And such epistemologies
continue to inspire refugee art

00:48:19.771 --> 00:48:23.390
and diasporic art media and criticism.

00:48:23.390 --> 00:48:27.310
And it behooves us to look and listen in.

00:48:27.310 --> 00:48:28.143
Thank you.

00:48:31.900 --> 00:48:34.643
- Thank you Lan, so much
for this wonderful talk.

00:48:35.600 --> 00:48:37.420
So we will now open the,

00:48:37.420 --> 00:48:39.250
open it up to Q&amp;A.

00:48:39.250 --> 00:48:42.392
Let me see, I'm gonna
stop the screen share.

00:48:42.392 --> 00:48:43.225
- Yeah.

00:48:43.225 --> 00:48:45.053
- Or maybe you can do it on your side?

00:48:46.760 --> 00:48:47.880
So we can see everybody.

00:48:47.880 --> 00:48:49.513
- Okay, nice.

00:48:50.900 --> 00:48:52.910
- I'm going to say in
advance that my computer

00:48:52.910 --> 00:48:56.150
just crashed a moment at the
very beginning of the talk.

00:48:56.150 --> 00:48:59.349
So if I disappear, keep talking
and I'll try to get back on.

00:48:59.349 --> 00:49:00.182
- Okay.

00:49:01.230 --> 00:49:03.870
- But so basically anyone
who has a question,

00:49:03.870 --> 00:49:05.570
you can use the hand raise function

00:49:05.570 --> 00:49:07.100
and I'll call on you and create a queue.

00:49:07.100 --> 00:49:09.213
And then we'll proceed from there.

00:49:13.740 --> 00:49:15.540
Quan, you have a question, go ahead.

00:49:17.090 --> 00:49:21.050
- Thanks so much Lan for
this very generative talk.

00:49:21.050 --> 00:49:24.510
I was really struck by your praising

00:49:24.510 --> 00:49:29.510
of how refugee memories, you
know, emerge and facilitate

00:49:30.090 --> 00:49:35.090
and are on display in
unspectacular places.

00:49:36.930 --> 00:49:38.843
And I think of, you know,

00:49:38.843 --> 00:49:40.867
there's also in the context of water

00:49:40.867 --> 00:49:43.020
in the Vietnamese context,

00:49:43.020 --> 00:49:46.560
water can also be very spectacular, right?

00:49:46.560 --> 00:49:48.550
So I'm thinking about flood.

00:49:48.550 --> 00:49:53.280
I'm thinking about the
salinization of fresh water

00:49:54.180 --> 00:49:59.180
and those are also sites of
refugee producing dynamics.

00:49:59.558 --> 00:50:02.220
So I was just wondering if you could

00:50:02.220 --> 00:50:04.990
maybe speak a little bit
more on sort of this,

00:50:04.990 --> 00:50:09.205
you know, water as a
trope, water as a metaphor,

00:50:09.205 --> 00:50:12.560
but also the differentiation
between different

00:50:12.560 --> 00:50:17.070
kinds of water, if that
resonate in your work.

00:50:17.070 --> 00:50:18.430
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:50:18.430 --> 00:50:19.990
Thank you Quan.

00:50:19.990 --> 00:50:24.990
So in my writing, I've talked
about water in its more,

00:50:25.810 --> 00:50:29.222
I guess, generative and productive modes

00:50:29.222 --> 00:50:31.430
as a space of loss.

00:50:31.430 --> 00:50:35.200
But from that loss, the
generation of memories

00:50:35.200 --> 00:50:37.050
and stories and knowledges.

00:50:37.050 --> 00:50:40.480
In other ways, water can be

00:50:40.480 --> 00:50:43.580
and I wanted to pull out that
tension a little bit more,

00:50:43.580 --> 00:50:45.940
water can be very threatening,

00:50:45.940 --> 00:50:50.720
water especially in Vietnamese
horror films, (chuckles)

00:50:50.720 --> 00:50:54.350
which I claim to have
knowledge on actually,

00:50:54.350 --> 00:50:58.690
because I've taught Asian horror
films and write about them.

00:50:58.690 --> 00:51:02.280
Water is a dangerous element,

00:51:02.280 --> 00:51:06.340
to be submerged in water
and to die in the water

00:51:06.340 --> 00:51:08.205
is to be away from one's home,

00:51:08.205 --> 00:51:12.000
ancestral home and to be bloated.

00:51:12.000 --> 00:51:15.130
And so that is something to be feared

00:51:15.130 --> 00:51:20.130
and it's a horrifying aspect
of some of the horror films

00:51:20.180 --> 00:51:21.580
that I've looked at.

00:51:21.580 --> 00:51:23.550
And in so, in terms of flooding,

00:51:23.550 --> 00:51:27.667
which is a very important,

00:51:28.650 --> 00:51:31.610
critical fact of Vietnamese life,

00:51:31.610 --> 00:51:36.353
I would love to think about
its productive properties

00:51:38.570 --> 00:51:43.570
and the ways in which it
actually brings people together.

00:51:44.080 --> 00:51:47.640
And I don't know if this
is going in the direction

00:51:47.640 --> 00:51:50.450
that you wanted me to
take you to, (chuckles)

00:51:50.450 --> 00:51:54.843
but there is a comedy or
a movie called Booyah.

00:51:56.400 --> 00:51:57.790
And I recently seen it,

00:51:57.790 --> 00:52:02.790
unfortunately (chuckles)
because I have a tendency

00:52:03.050 --> 00:52:04.740
to watch bad Vietnamese films.

00:52:04.740 --> 00:52:08.300
But so in the setting of that film,

00:52:08.300 --> 00:52:12.800
the family comes together
because then they're in an alley

00:52:12.800 --> 00:52:17.206
and the film has scenes of flooding

00:52:17.206 --> 00:52:21.202
within this family community space.

00:52:21.202 --> 00:52:26.020
And it's that water that
brings them together.

00:52:26.020 --> 00:52:30.580
And as the film continues,
it's what brings them together,

00:52:30.580 --> 00:52:33.363
bonds them together as a unit.

00:52:34.400 --> 00:52:38.370
But also as the story goes,

00:52:38.370 --> 00:52:43.370
also the family unit is broken
apart by these lingering

00:52:43.600 --> 00:52:46.240
resentments and family bonds.

00:52:46.240 --> 00:52:49.050
So I think that there
are ways we could argue

00:52:49.050 --> 00:52:53.160
for how water can be very multivalent

00:52:53.160 --> 00:52:56.590
in lived experiences in Vietnam,

00:52:56.590 --> 00:52:58.670
Vietnamese lived experiences.

00:52:58.670 --> 00:53:01.513
But then also how allegorically in film,

00:53:01.513 --> 00:53:03.580
and for me aesthetically,

00:53:03.580 --> 00:53:08.580
how water acts as an agent
in many ways to conjure,

00:53:09.580 --> 00:53:14.013
to act as a mnemonic device
to remember and to grieve.

00:53:15.700 --> 00:53:16.533
Thank you, Quen.

00:53:20.181 --> 00:53:21.660
- I'm gonna jump in on that

00:53:21.660 --> 00:53:25.905
because I was thinking
during the whole presentation

00:53:25.905 --> 00:53:28.810
about (indistinct)

00:53:31.460 --> 00:53:33.140
because that film,

00:53:33.140 --> 00:53:36.076
I think actually gives you
a really great opportunity

00:53:36.076 --> 00:53:40.500
to speak about that
double meaning of flood

00:53:40.500 --> 00:53:44.930
as both like disaster and regeneration,

00:53:44.930 --> 00:53:46.369
'cause the whole setting of that film

00:53:46.369 --> 00:53:50.090
is about the way in which the floods come,

00:53:50.090 --> 00:53:53.440
but they also create the
opportunity for, you know,

00:53:53.440 --> 00:53:55.900
regeneration of life and
all those kinds of things,

00:53:55.900 --> 00:53:57.430
but it creates this kind of

00:53:57.430 --> 00:53:59.363
traumatic setting as well, right?

00:54:00.240 --> 00:54:02.640
And I think the history
of the Mekong Delta

00:54:02.640 --> 00:54:07.070
in many ways kind of actually
stereotypically sometimes

00:54:07.070 --> 00:54:08.910
even Northern Vietnamese
when they will talk

00:54:08.910 --> 00:54:11.440
about the Mekong Delta
as this kind of place

00:54:11.440 --> 00:54:13.430
of immense fertility, you know,

00:54:13.430 --> 00:54:16.385
it's a huge stereotype,
but it's persistent, right?

00:54:16.385 --> 00:54:17.550
They used to say like,

00:54:17.550 --> 00:54:18.637
you can go to the Mekong Delta

00:54:18.637 --> 00:54:21.350
and just stick your hand in the
water and you'll get a fish.

00:54:21.350 --> 00:54:25.720
And that's why Southern
Vietnamese people are, you know,

00:54:25.720 --> 00:54:26.750
have an easier life

00:54:26.750 --> 00:54:28.760
than Northern Vietnamese
people or something.

00:54:28.760 --> 00:54:29.840
But at the same time,

00:54:29.840 --> 00:54:32.640
there's also this deep history of flooding

00:54:32.640 --> 00:54:34.770
also the future-

00:54:34.770 --> 00:54:38.710
- [Male Voiceover] Thank
you for calling (indistinct)

00:54:38.710 --> 00:54:40.263
- Is under threat.

00:54:41.741 --> 00:54:42.610
- Right.

00:54:42.610 --> 00:54:43.570
- [Erik] So that, I just thought that'd

00:54:43.570 --> 00:54:45.860
be a really interesting angle to look at

00:54:45.860 --> 00:54:47.490
and also his follow-up film Nuoc,

00:54:47.490 --> 00:54:51.030
but I forget the year
that's 2030 or something.

00:54:51.030 --> 00:54:52.410
- 2030. Mmh-hmm.

00:54:52.410 --> 00:54:55.700
- Yeah, so Nuoc 2030 also has this kind of

00:54:55.700 --> 00:54:56.943
double edge to it.

00:54:58.320 --> 00:55:03.320
But my question for you is
about the temporality of this,

00:55:04.080 --> 00:55:07.630
this phrase you used when you
were talking about the sea

00:55:07.630 --> 00:55:12.630
as becoming amniotic,
as opposed to traumatic.

00:55:12.780 --> 00:55:15.531
And I say temporality
'cause I'm wondering,

00:55:15.531 --> 00:55:19.590
does it make sense to say
there's a historical shift

00:55:19.590 --> 00:55:22.240
in the symbolic meaning of the sea?

00:55:22.240 --> 00:55:27.240
Like for example, because you
know, immediately after 75,

00:55:27.810 --> 00:55:31.870
the sea can have a kind
of traumatic symbolism,

00:55:31.870 --> 00:55:34.291
but then perhaps historically over time,

00:55:34.291 --> 00:55:36.290
the meaning of the sea

00:55:36.290 --> 00:55:39.260
becomes recuperated in some kind of way.

00:55:39.260 --> 00:55:41.910
And or is it just sort
of a flexible symbol

00:55:41.910 --> 00:55:45.220
that can change in the
hands of a different artist

00:55:45.220 --> 00:55:46.540
or something like that.

00:55:46.540 --> 00:55:47.470
- Right. I think.

00:55:47.470 --> 00:55:50.664
And so I'm gonna agree
with your last final,

00:55:50.664 --> 00:55:55.550
your second point about it's
fluidity and it's flexibility

00:55:55.550 --> 00:56:00.550
to hold different kinds of
allegorical and critical import.

00:56:01.440 --> 00:56:05.560
And I'm not arguing that then water

00:56:05.560 --> 00:56:09.169
is a temporal or a historic,

00:56:09.169 --> 00:56:12.160
but I am looking very specifically

00:56:12.160 --> 00:56:14.785
about how Vietnamese diaspora

00:56:14.785 --> 00:56:19.785
and refugees are using
this element of water

00:56:20.680 --> 00:56:24.790
to draw on it's historical
and cultural significance

00:56:24.790 --> 00:56:28.712
in Vietnamese culture and in
Vietnamese lived experiences,

00:56:28.712 --> 00:56:32.653
but also to focus on the,

00:56:35.090 --> 00:56:39.290
how the diaspora understands,
I think through water,

00:56:39.290 --> 00:56:42.190
what home and home land mean.

00:56:42.190 --> 00:56:47.190
So once it's flexible, on the other hand,

00:56:49.650 --> 00:56:53.670
how I'm understanding water
for the Vietnamese diaspora

00:56:53.670 --> 00:56:55.770
filmmakers that I look at,

00:56:55.770 --> 00:56:59.950
they're very specific to how water seems

00:56:59.950 --> 00:57:03.310
to contain secrets and knowledge as well

00:57:03.310 --> 00:57:07.603
as the actual dead bodies of refugees.

00:57:09.560 --> 00:57:14.350
And I'm happy to see that
(indistinct) is here,

00:57:14.350 --> 00:57:19.150
I don't wanna call her out,
but her brother did both films.

00:57:19.150 --> 00:57:23.563
And so happy to see
you. It's been so long.

00:57:25.370 --> 00:57:27.590
And I do talk about Nuoc 2030.

00:57:27.590 --> 00:57:29.680
I think it's a beautiful film.

00:57:29.680 --> 00:57:32.991
And speaking of temporality then,

00:57:32.991 --> 00:57:37.991
the fact that it's so centrally
placed in a scientific,

00:57:38.880 --> 00:57:41.780
or sci-fi imaginary of
(speaking in foreign language)

00:57:41.780 --> 00:57:43.946
being submerged by the sea.

00:57:43.946 --> 00:57:48.946
I think there is so much
to talk about in that act,

00:57:49.150 --> 00:57:52.870
in that actuality of being flooded,

00:57:52.870 --> 00:57:55.656
the actuality of environmental harm

00:57:55.656 --> 00:57:58.040
and the perhaps, you know,

00:57:58.040 --> 00:58:03.040
the generative notion of
water as a way to understand

00:58:03.800 --> 00:58:08.200
a kind of futurity that
hasn't really been,

00:58:08.200 --> 00:58:11.310
I think dealt with in some
of the Vietnamese films

00:58:11.310 --> 00:58:12.143
that I've seen.

00:58:12.143 --> 00:58:15.910
So it is definitely
important to talk about

00:58:18.040 --> 00:58:20.593
Minh's work in this context as well.

00:58:22.910 --> 00:58:26.393
- Thanks, there's another
question from Tien Nguyen.

00:58:29.210 --> 00:58:30.440
- Hi professor Lan.

00:58:30.440 --> 00:58:32.693
Thank you for your presentations.

00:58:34.100 --> 00:58:37.941
My questions for you is
that I see in four clips

00:58:37.941 --> 00:58:40.940
that you present in your presentations,

00:58:40.940 --> 00:58:43.300
the like of urban scapes.

00:58:43.300 --> 00:58:47.480
And as I would wonder if the relationship

00:58:47.480 --> 00:58:49.053
between the symbolic water

00:58:49.053 --> 00:58:53.100
and the water body has something related

00:58:53.100 --> 00:58:57.380
with the more underdeveloped past

00:58:57.380 --> 00:59:01.070
of Vietnamese contemporary,
Vietnamese society,

00:59:01.070 --> 00:59:03.880
to the diasporic community focus

00:59:03.880 --> 00:59:07.740
on how to, portraying
water as something related

00:59:07.740 --> 00:59:12.740
with poverty and economics
under development or not,

00:59:14.070 --> 00:59:17.700
or if there's any other
interpretations on the water

00:59:17.700 --> 00:59:20.950
relating with urban
scapes and urbanizations.

00:59:20.950 --> 00:59:21.783
Thank you.

00:59:25.657 --> 00:59:30.657
- So you noticed Nien
that there are scenes

00:59:32.020 --> 00:59:36.458
of urban spaces in
perhaps a film, like Nuoc,

00:59:36.458 --> 00:59:41.420
like Quyen Nguyen-Le's but
there's also where they are

00:59:41.420 --> 00:59:46.420
in a suburban home
finally, eating together

00:59:46.910 --> 00:59:51.910
and ending on that note of offering chao

00:59:52.490 --> 00:59:55.130
to Rosie's friend, queer friend.

00:59:55.130 --> 00:59:58.660
And then also in Khoa Do's Mother Fish,

00:59:58.660 --> 01:00:03.660
we begin with the older
sister Gim preparing food

01:00:04.110 --> 01:00:05.380
within the kitchen.

01:00:05.380 --> 01:00:08.180
Is that, are these the spaces
that you're talking about?

01:00:11.580 --> 01:00:13.100
- Yes.

01:00:13.100 --> 01:00:17.013
Well then I also think
of entertainments has

01:00:17.013 --> 01:00:20.923
for what your clip's showing it's that

01:00:20.923 --> 01:00:22.894
when she's returning to Vietnam

01:00:22.894 --> 01:00:25.070
or when she filmed it in Vietnam,

01:00:25.070 --> 01:00:27.240
the waters and the lands,

01:00:27.240 --> 01:00:32.240
kinda like having the sense
of like the more rural spaces

01:00:34.620 --> 01:00:38.580
rather than the urban spaces
and Tran Anh Hung's films

01:00:38.580 --> 01:00:41.063
is basically in a refugee camp.

01:00:42.090 --> 01:00:44.061
- Yeah, and a Parisian apartment.

01:00:44.061 --> 01:00:47.060
So yeah, there is that duality,

01:00:47.060 --> 01:00:52.060
I think of being in both land and water.

01:00:54.140 --> 01:00:57.880
I think that's part of the
dialectic that Trinh T. Minh-ha

01:00:57.880 --> 01:01:00.430
wants to emphasize into the work.

01:01:00.430 --> 01:01:03.560
And I think that the
observation that you make

01:01:03.560 --> 01:01:06.350
about these different spaces is key

01:01:06.350 --> 01:01:11.350
to my larger argument about
how refugees reimagine

01:01:11.969 --> 01:01:16.969
in very particular ways,
how the boat passage has,

01:01:19.597 --> 01:01:22.580
how they have experienced the boat passage

01:01:22.580 --> 01:01:24.990
through actual lived memories

01:01:24.990 --> 01:01:28.510
or through post memories
and how then there's

01:01:28.510 --> 01:01:30.710
a continuity from that space.

01:01:30.710 --> 01:01:33.030
Like for example, in the refugee camp,

01:01:33.030 --> 01:01:36.050
to the Parisian apartment,

01:01:36.050 --> 01:01:41.050
there's a continuity between
forms of waiting in that camp

01:01:41.090 --> 01:01:44.740
to forms of waiting in
the apartment building.

01:01:44.740 --> 01:01:47.520
And in fact, the apartment
building that we see

01:01:47.520 --> 01:01:49.290
is actually less dreary,

01:01:49.290 --> 01:01:52.720
is much more dreary than the refugee camp.

01:01:52.720 --> 01:01:55.844
And so that juxtaposition
of these different spaces,

01:01:55.844 --> 01:01:58.360
I think allows us to see the kinds of

01:01:58.360 --> 01:02:02.940
psychological states too, of their,

01:02:02.940 --> 01:02:06.310
of refugee subjectivities
and their memories,

01:02:06.310 --> 01:02:10.330
and how the associations
between the home life

01:02:10.330 --> 01:02:14.923
and workspace are really
flooded to use a term

01:02:16.450 --> 01:02:20.070
we talked about before,
flooded with memories

01:02:20.070 --> 01:02:24.840
of the boat passage, of
refugee life, of refugee death.

01:02:24.840 --> 01:02:27.473
So thank you Nien for that observation.

01:02:30.560 --> 01:02:31.510
Hi Qui Ha

01:02:33.160 --> 01:02:34.163
- Hi Lan.

01:02:35.030 --> 01:02:38.370
Thank you so much for your
wonderful presentation

01:02:38.370 --> 01:02:42.360
on the topic refugees cinema,

01:02:42.360 --> 01:02:46.140
which is just amazing
because in film studies,

01:02:46.140 --> 01:02:49.050
it's just a bit more than one,

01:02:49.050 --> 01:02:51.690
the first, second, and
third cinema. (chuckles)

01:02:51.690 --> 01:02:54.529
And now you talk about the fifth cinema.

01:02:54.529 --> 01:02:57.570
I'm so glad to learn more about it

01:02:57.570 --> 01:02:59.540
and learn more about, you know,

01:02:59.540 --> 01:03:02.749
outside the first and
the second, (chuckles)

01:03:02.749 --> 01:03:07.040
which is great to explore
that kind of possibilities

01:03:07.040 --> 01:03:12.040
to think of cinema outside
of western oriented

01:03:15.075 --> 01:03:17.242
or based theories.

01:03:17.242 --> 01:03:22.242
So I really appreciate,
and I'm very, you know,

01:03:22.844 --> 01:03:27.844
inspired by your discussion of water

01:03:28.090 --> 01:03:32.103
as a troop in diasporic filmmakers films.

01:03:33.133 --> 01:03:35.970
And also the ways that is represented

01:03:35.970 --> 01:03:40.970
as a archive of refugees memories.

01:03:41.380 --> 01:03:43.810
I have a particular question in terms of

01:03:45.700 --> 01:03:48.360
not only the representation,
but I'm curious,

01:03:48.360 --> 01:03:53.360
like if you, in your work
broader, you know, project,

01:03:53.490 --> 01:03:56.700
would you discuss anything
about, for example,

01:03:56.700 --> 01:04:01.700
the kind of like, this is
not just the representation,

01:04:01.800 --> 01:04:06.800
but the way that cinema is worse actually

01:04:07.100 --> 01:04:11.710
brought to from the country
to outside of the country.

01:04:11.710 --> 01:04:15.440
Because like recently,
I think two years ago,

01:04:15.440 --> 01:04:17.280
I read a case study,

01:04:17.280 --> 01:04:19.810
I don't remember what is the film title,

01:04:19.810 --> 01:04:24.810
but it is about a film
that was lost on the ways

01:04:25.070 --> 01:04:27.290
a film maker brought it with him,

01:04:27.290 --> 01:04:30.220
but then it's lost somewhere
and then somebody took it

01:04:31.250 --> 01:04:33.310
and then later into Hong Kong.

01:04:33.310 --> 01:04:36.480
And then it's returned
to him in Australia.

01:04:36.480 --> 01:04:37.920
So I think that kind of,

01:04:37.920 --> 01:04:42.820
consider theme as an
object, which traveled,

01:04:42.820 --> 01:04:47.150
you know, on water as a, you know,

01:04:47.150 --> 01:04:50.514
this is more in the physical terms

01:04:50.514 --> 01:04:54.670
rather than a representational terms.

01:04:54.670 --> 01:04:57.319
I mean, like that part
it's wonderful already,

01:04:57.319 --> 01:05:02.319
so I'm wondering if you, you
know, consider in that sense.

01:05:02.350 --> 01:05:05.180
And also the second
question I'm curious is,

01:05:05.180 --> 01:05:09.960
you discussed the representation
in diasporic films,

01:05:13.130 --> 01:05:18.130
and I'm wondering if you discuss
it within the connections

01:05:19.891 --> 01:05:24.891
to the Vietnamese film
tradition or only focus

01:05:25.232 --> 01:05:30.232
on that part, like
Jeffrey's cinema and yeah,

01:05:32.412 --> 01:05:34.270
that is the questions that I have.

01:05:34.270 --> 01:05:35.213
Thank you.

01:05:36.280 --> 01:05:39.660
- I really like the first question.

01:05:39.660 --> 01:05:40.580
I like the second too,

01:05:40.580 --> 01:05:45.580
but the first question about
the materiality of films

01:05:46.520 --> 01:05:49.793
and how they too have traveled on sea,

01:05:50.927 --> 01:05:53.913
at sea and through waterways.

01:05:54.950 --> 01:05:58.590
I think that's something
that I want to look about,

01:05:58.590 --> 01:06:01.060
look into a lot more closely

01:06:01.060 --> 01:06:03.660
because some of the films that were made

01:06:03.660 --> 01:06:06.600
in pre '75 Saigon for example,

01:06:06.600 --> 01:06:11.300
did have to make it outside
of the country in '75.

01:06:11.300 --> 01:06:15.320
And I can think of a couple of examples,

01:06:15.320 --> 01:06:18.340
for example, with Qiu Jin's work,

01:06:18.340 --> 01:06:23.340
who left Vietnam in '75
and who had to transport

01:06:23.540 --> 01:06:28.540
some of her beloved films with her.

01:06:28.550 --> 01:06:30.680
I don't think she went by sea,

01:06:30.680 --> 01:06:35.680
but it was a mode of
travel in which she felt

01:06:36.210 --> 01:06:39.560
like these were material
and cultural artifacts

01:06:39.560 --> 01:06:43.260
that needed to be saved.

01:06:43.260 --> 01:06:48.170
Another mode of travel through airplanes

01:06:48.170 --> 01:06:51.170
that Vietnamese films
have traveled through

01:06:51.170 --> 01:06:53.340
is when the Library of Congress,

01:06:53.340 --> 01:06:54.173
oh, I'm sorry.

01:06:54.173 --> 01:06:57.755
When the American government
in withdrawing from Vietnam

01:06:57.755 --> 01:07:00.220
also took a lot of films,

01:07:00.220 --> 01:07:02.770
especially done by the US government

01:07:02.770 --> 01:07:05.503
and brought them to the
Library of Congress.

01:07:05.503 --> 01:07:10.503
And that's where a lot of
these films are archived.

01:07:11.620 --> 01:07:14.708
And then finally, in the
more contemporary version

01:07:14.708 --> 01:07:19.708
of traveling films, we now
have a very vibrant passageway

01:07:24.190 --> 01:07:25.970
between Vietnam and the diaspora

01:07:25.970 --> 01:07:28.904
in terms of films, whereby Vietnamese,

01:07:28.904 --> 01:07:32.290
the Vietnam Film Festival
in Orange County,

01:07:32.290 --> 01:07:37.290
often invites directors
and films from Vietnam

01:07:37.660 --> 01:07:39.440
to screen their works.

01:07:39.440 --> 01:07:41.580
And we have then another passage

01:07:41.580 --> 01:07:43.710
of Vietnamese American directors,

01:07:43.710 --> 01:07:45.730
for example, from California,

01:07:45.730 --> 01:07:49.100
who go to Saigon
specifically to make films.

01:07:49.100 --> 01:07:50.970
So in this context,

01:07:50.970 --> 01:07:54.920
films and filmmakers are
constantly circulating.

01:07:54.920 --> 01:07:57.523
And so that's an interesting geography

01:07:59.885 --> 01:08:04.670
that I'm, that I've
looked into in my work.

01:08:04.670 --> 01:08:08.150
So, and it relates to the
second question we have,

01:08:08.150 --> 01:08:12.220
because this is where
my method of analysis

01:08:12.220 --> 01:08:17.220
trans-Vietnamese feminism
tries to put into place.

01:08:17.240 --> 01:08:19.070
And that is if I'm looking at something

01:08:19.070 --> 01:08:21.417
in terms of the Vietnamese diaspora,

01:08:21.417 --> 01:08:25.310
I'd like to see how too
Vietnamese filmmakers

01:08:25.310 --> 01:08:29.190
have looked at or represented water.

01:08:29.190 --> 01:08:31.970
So as you know, there is

01:08:34.530 --> 01:08:38.686
along the same river of
the famous, you know,

01:08:38.686 --> 01:08:43.686
film of two lovers who are
separated at the 17th parallel

01:08:45.050 --> 01:08:49.420
between north and south,
they are surrounded by water.

01:08:49.420 --> 01:08:52.947
And I talk about how
that kind of passageway

01:08:55.380 --> 01:08:59.880
between north and south peasants,

01:08:59.880 --> 01:09:03.950
because they're also fishers
are treated differently

01:09:03.950 --> 01:09:07.390
in a communist film like that.

01:09:07.390 --> 01:09:10.560
And so, and water has more

01:09:10.560 --> 01:09:15.560
of a kind of pragmatic way of travel.

01:09:17.430 --> 01:09:21.870
We see a lot of traveling on the women

01:09:23.390 --> 01:09:26.543
on the perfume river, for
example, by (indistinct).

01:09:26.543 --> 01:09:29.690
And she uses it as also a way to

01:09:31.460 --> 01:09:35.650
it's a space for her sex work on the boat.

01:09:35.650 --> 01:09:39.937
And so finally in another
film called (indistinct).

01:09:43.830 --> 01:09:47.649
The woman who's just been
raped, played by (indistinct)

01:09:47.649 --> 01:09:52.649
goes to the ocean, and she's
about to commit suicide.

01:09:52.870 --> 01:09:57.460
And then she turns around because
she hears her baby crying.

01:09:57.460 --> 01:10:00.580
And it's at this point
of this very poignant,

01:10:00.580 --> 01:10:04.960
pivotal moment where she
realizes that she will mother

01:10:04.960 --> 01:10:08.570
the country and mother, and
continue to mother her child.

01:10:08.570 --> 01:10:10.150
At that moment,

01:10:10.150 --> 01:10:12.295
we get a juxtaposition of the ocean

01:10:12.295 --> 01:10:16.460
plus her breast and she
is breastfeeding the baby.

01:10:16.460 --> 01:10:19.650
So there are different ways
in which Vietnamese directors

01:10:19.650 --> 01:10:22.420
have understood water and ocean.

01:10:22.420 --> 01:10:25.830
And it's in my writing that
I try to tease out those

01:10:25.830 --> 01:10:29.783
important differences
and attachments to water.

01:10:31.460 --> 01:10:32.378
- Thanks Qin Ha.

01:10:32.378 --> 01:10:33.870
- It's wonderful. Thank you so much.

01:10:33.870 --> 01:10:35.270
I look for what you're reading

01:10:35.270 --> 01:10:38.680
your writings on this topic very much.

01:10:38.680 --> 01:10:39.513
- Thanks.

01:10:43.600 --> 01:10:45.741
- We're basically at the
end of our time here,

01:10:45.741 --> 01:10:48.470
usually what I do is I
stop the recording now,

01:10:48.470 --> 01:10:50.350
and then we keep it open for a few minutes

01:10:50.350 --> 01:10:51.680
for informal chitchat.

01:10:51.680 --> 01:10:53.120
So let's all,

01:10:53.120 --> 01:10:54.760
join me in a round of applause

01:10:54.760 --> 01:10:56.164
and then I'll stop the recording.

01:10:56.164 --> 01:10:57.019
(people clapping)

01:10:57.019 --> 01:10:58.367
- (chuckles) Thank you.

